<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herodium</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herodium is a fortified palace built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a> on an artificially heightened, cone-shaped hill south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a>. Herod chose it as his burial place, and in 2007 archaeologists led by Ehud Netzer identified what they believed to be his tomb on its slopes. Jewish rebels held it in the revolts against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> of 66–73 and 132–135.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herodium.jpg" alt="Herodium" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Asaf T. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herodium_from_above_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
